  "slug": "fields-spatial-transcriptomics-d7eb",
  "summary": "New methods remove batch effects and background noise in mass cytometry while spatial and single-cell models handle domain, donor and sample variation.",
  "synthesis": "The most coherent thread is computational removal of unwanted variation in high-dimensional single-cell measurements. mapfx.norm removes background noise and batch effects from massively parallel cytometry data, and BioBatchNet uses a dual-encoder adversarial design to separate batch from biological signal in imaging mass cytometry, enabling controllable correction. Around this core, several items apply latent-variable or attention models to related variation in spatial and transcriptomic data: SpatialMuSC (graph-conditioned diffusion refinement for multi-omics integration), GASTON-VI (multi-sample spatial axes), GLAND (small, non-continuous domains), and scPACE (cell and donor states in cohorts). Space-map aligns serial sections into 3D atlases, and Bulk2SC generates scRNA-seq data from bulk RNA-seq. The new element is explicit disentanglement of technical from biological signal, mostly through deep generative or adversarial architectures.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
